# Signing values for one build environment. Copy environments/example once
# for each environment into $ONETIMEPAD_ENVIRONMENTS_DIR, by default
# ~/.local/appledev/CompanionApp/environments, and rename this file to .env in
# each copy:
#
#   dev      scripts/dev.sh and scripts/package-app.sh --debug.
#            Bundle identifier: dev.onetimesecret.pad.debug
#   local    scripts/install.sh and scripts/package-app.sh with no lane flag.
#            Bundle identifier: dev.onetimesecret.pad
#   staging  scripts/package-app.sh --app-store, as built for TestFlight.
#            Bundle identifier: com.onetimesecret.pad
#
# The names are the same in every copy; the directory says which lane the
# values belong to. A lane takes them from its own file and nowhere else:
# values of these names inherited from the shell are discarded before the file
# is read.
#
# The packaging scripts source this file with bash, and the .envrc beside it
# loads it with direnv's `dotenv`, so keep to KEY="value" lines; both readers
# expand $HOME and ${HOME} alike.

# dev and local: "Apple Development: Name (TEAMID)"
# staging:       "3rd Party Mac Developer Application: Name (TEAMID)"
#
# CODESIGN_IDENTITY="Apple Development: Name (TEAMID)"

# The profile downloaded into this environment directory, for example
# dev/OnetimePad_Dev.provisionprofile, local/OnetimePad_Local.provisionprofile,
# or staging/OnetimePad_App_Store.provisionprofile.
#
# PROVISIONING_PROFILE="$HOME/.local/appledev/CompanionApp/environments/<environment>/<profile>.provisionprofile"

# staging only; the dev and local lanes ignore it.
#
# INSTALLER_IDENTITY="3rd Party Mac Developer Installer: Name (TEAMID)"

# dev and local both use a macOS development certificate and a macOS
# development provisioning profile authorizing that certificate, this Mac, and
# the lane's bundle identifier. Explicit App IDs take one profile per lane; a
# wildcard TEAMID.dev.onetimesecret.* development profile may cover both.
#
# The provisioning profile is required only when the build carries
# scripts/Companion.entitlements. Without a profile, a configured identity
# still signs the app but the script omits those restricted entitlements. To
# create a development profile at developer.apple.com:
#
#   1. Register an explicit macOS App ID for the bundle identifier, or use a
#      wildcard macOS App ID when the required capabilities permit it.
#   2. Register this Mac's Provisioning UDID (About This Mac, System Report,
#      Hardware, Provisioning UDID).
#   3. Create a macOS App Development profile for the App ID, development
#      certificate, and this Mac.
#   4. Download the profile into this environment directory and set
#      PROVISIONING_PROFILE above.
#
# docs/development/development-profiles.md has the same steps with the portal
# URLs for each.
#
# Before compiling or replacing a bundle, package-app.sh verifies the profile's
# team, App ID, Keychain group, certificate, expiry, profile class, and this
# Mac's Provisioning UDID.
#
# staging requires all three values. Use the exact identity names reported by
# `security find-identity` and an explicit Mac App Store distribution profile
# for com.onetimesecret.pad. Before compiling or replacing a bundle,
# package-app.sh verifies the profile's team, App ID, Keychain group,
# certificate, expiry, and profile class.
#
# Optional App Store build number counter, staging only. The default is
# onetimepad-app-store-build-number in the git common directory, shared by every
# worktree of one clone, and this variable is required outside a git checkout.
# A path in this directory is shared by every clone on this Mac.
#
# APP_STORE_BUILD_NUMBER_FILE="$HOME/.local/appledev/CompanionApp/environments/staging/app-store-build-number"
